ROLE: novelty_killer  VOTE: yellow

CLOSEST_PRIOR_WORK:
- {cite: "pandas docs — Categorical.codes ('codes are the positions of the actual values in the categories array')", url: "https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/reference/api/pandas.Categorical.codes.html", why_close: "Documents the EXACT mechanism: codes are positional into a per-object categories array that is sorted by default. The cross-shard divergence is the direct, documented consequence of this — the mechanism itself is not new."}
- {cite: "StackOverflow Q54731396 — categorical columns across multiple parquet files", url: "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/54731396", why_close: "Community treats cross-file categorical/code inconsistency as a real, known pain point with workarounds — i.e. the '.cat.codes isn't portable across files' folklore already circulates."}
- {cite: "pandas #44099 — BUG: pd.concat produces inconsistent order with categorical indices; #22143 concat with categorical variables", url: "https://github.com/pandas-dev/pandas/issues/44099", why_close: "Closest issue-tracker items, but scoped to concat-time alignment/ordering of categorical indices, NOT to separately-cast shards silently setting a divergent .cat.codes ML feature contract."}

NAMING_VS_MECHANISM: Primarily a FRAMING/COMPOSITION delta over a fully-documented mechanism. The codes-sorted-over-observed-values behavior and its non-portability are documented (codes are positional) and circulate as folklore (SO workarounds). The genuine-but-thin delta is the consequence framing — demonstrating it as a SILENT cross-shard ML feature-contract corruption with an exact partial-observation null boundary (f=0 → deltaR2=0) and monotone deltaR2. That specific framing/demonstration was not found in any source; the underlying mechanism is not new.

FATAL_COLLISIONS: None verified. Critically, I did NOT find a triaged pandas/pyarrow issue or merged PR owning "cross-shard .cat.codes-as-feature-contract instability" — so this does NOT replicate the CLAIM-0066 issue-tracker kill (no fix/triage collision to make it RED). The mechanism being documented is working-as-intended, not a bug maintainers would patch, so no fix-collision exists.

MANDATORY_RELATED_WORK:
- pandas Categorical.codes / Categorical-data user guide (must cite as the documented mechanism; authors must NOT claim mechanism novelty).
- The cross-file ".cat.codes not portable" folklore (SO Q54731396 and similar) — must be cited and the contribution positioned as composition-over-documented-behavior.
- pyarrow dictionary encoding first-seen-order docs (2nd arm) — must cite the documented dictionary-encode ordering.
- Kapoor & Narayanan 2023 (leakage taxonomy) — to explicitly disclaim the no-fitted-estimator distinction.

RATIONALE: Real prior-art search across ≥2 independent sources (pandas docs, SO, pandas issue tracker) confirms the MECHANISM is documented and the non-portability is community folklore — so this is not GREEN-grade mechanism novelty; it sits at the CLAIM-0064-style "documented gotcha" altitude on its mechanism leg. However, the specific cross-shard silent-ML-feature-contract framing with an exact f=0 null boundary has no owning issue/PR and was not found as a documented ML-hazard warning, and the non-obviousness defense (A emits strings only; B's codes are stable within-file so per-file docs don't fire the cross-shard alarm; B never fits an encoder) is coherent — giving it a thin but real framing delta. Because there is NO triaged-issue/fix collision (the CLAIM-0066 RED trigger is absent) but the mechanism is documented folklore (so not novel as a mechanism), the honest novelty verdict is YELLOW, conditional on the authors citing the documented codes-positional behavior + portability folklore and framing the contribution as composition, not discovery.
